= Anise (Erva-doce)

_Greco-Roman / European: fully classical — anise (ánison / anisum) is one of the most-treated spice-drugs of Dioscorides and Pliny, and one of the four "European stomachics" of the medieval and modern Western pharmacy. Ayurveda: not strictly classical — anise (Pimpinella anisum) is Mediterranean and was not the saṃhitā-named śatapuṣpā / madhurikā (those are fennel / dill); the modern Sanskrit / Hindi_ saunf / vilāyatī saunf _is post-classical naming for a similar drug. Chinese: not strictly classical for_ Pimpinella anisum_; the canonical Chinese 八角 / 大茴香 is_ star anise (Illicium verum) _— a different plant in a different family (Schisandraceae, formerly Illiciaceae). The user-facing Brazilian-Portuguese "erva-doce" is ambiguous (sometimes anise, sometimes fennel); the distinction below disambiguates._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin:* _Pimpinella anisum_ L., family Apiaceae (Umbelliferae); the drug is the dried *ripe fruit* (a schizocarp), with the *essential oil* (Oleum Anisi / Anisi aetheroleum) as the principal pharmaceutical form. _Pimpinella_ — possibly from Late Latin _bipinella_ ("twice-pinnate," from the leaf shape); _anisum_ is direct from Greek ἄνισον (_ánison_) — a pre-Greek (substrate) word, of disputed further etymology; one tradition relates it to ἄνηθον (_ánēthon_, dill — a different plant in the same family, but with similar fruit), and indeed the ancient Greek lexicon was *not* always clearly distinguished between anise and dill (a real source of identification confusion in the Hippocratic and early-Dioscoridean corpus, only finally clarified by Dioscorides himself).#footnote[Etymology: Liddell & Scott, _A Greek–English Lexicon_ (s.v. _ἄνισον_, _ἄνηθον_); Beekes, _Etymological Dictionary of Greek_ (s.v. _ἄνισον_, pre-Greek substrate); Lewis & Short, _A Latin Dictionary_ (s.v. _anisum_, _pimpinella_). The ánison / ánēthon (anise / dill) confusion in earlier Greek pharmacy literature is well documented; Dioscorides (_De Materia Medica_ III.56, _ánison_; III.58, _ánēthon_) separates them explicitly and is the standard reference for the disambiguation.]

*English:* _Anise_ (also _aniseed_), from Old French _anis_, from Latin _anisum_, from Greek _ánison_. The compound _aniseed_ ("anise-seed") for the fruit / "seed" of the plant.#footnote[_Oxford English Dictionary_ (s.v. "anise, n.", "aniseed, n.").]

*Chinese:* The canonical Chinese spice-drug 八角茴香 / 大茴香 (bājiǎo huíxiāng / dàhuíxiāng) is *star anise*, _Illicium verum_ Hook.f., family Schisandraceae — *a completely different plant from European anise (Pimpinella anisum) in a different family.* The two share the *anethole* essential-oil base (~80–90% trans-anethole in both) and an organoleptic similarity (the "anise" flavor), but they are not the same species. Modern Chinese terms for the European Pimpinella anisum: 茴芹 (huíqín, "fennel-Apium"), 西洋茴香 / 洋茴香 (xīyáng huíxiāng / yáng huíxiāng, "Western fennel"), or the transliterated 大茴芹. The classical Chinese pharmacy used 八角 (star anise) and 小茴香 (fennel, _Foeniculum vulgare_); _Pimpinella anisum_ has no canonical Chinese entry.#footnote[Star anise / European anise distinction: Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Chinese Herbal Medicine: Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. (Eastland Press, 2004) — 八角茴香 (Illicium verum); _Flora of China_ on Schisandraceae and on Pimpinella; the 茴芹 modern Chinese term for European anise is a modern coinage. The anethole convergence (both Illicium verum and Pimpinella anisum yield trans-anethole as the dominant essential-oil compound) is the chemical basis of the organoleptic similarity but does not unify them taxonomically.]

*Sanskrit:* The substance was not native to South Asia in the saṃhitā period; the modern Sanskrit / Hindi names are post-classical:
- *शतपुष्पा (śatapuṣpā)* — classical name, *but* identified by some commentators as Anethum graveolens (dill) and by others as Foeniculum vulgare (fennel); *not* anise.
- *मधुरिका (madhurikā)*, "the sweet one" — modern Hindi _saunf_, classically applied to fennel (Foeniculum), not anise.
- *विलायती सौंफ (vilāyatī saunf)*, "foreign fennel" — modern Hindi for *Pimpinella anisum* (European anise), distinguishing it from the indigenous fennel.
- *सौंफ (saunf)* alone, in modern Hindi, normally refers to *fennel*; "anise" is _vilāyatī saunf_ to distinguish.
Honest: *Pimpinella anisum* has no classical Sanskrit identification; what looks like an Ayurvedic treatment of "anise" is usually a treatment of fennel or dill under the same Sanskrit name. The dravyaguna placement below is reasoned cross-system, anchored on the Mediterranean tradition and the modern phytochemistry — not citation.#footnote[The Sanskrit-name identification ambiguity for śatapuṣpā / madhurikā is real and well documented in the dravyaguna commentary literature; cf. P. V. Sharma, _Dravyaguṇa-vijñāna_, vol. II, who treats fennel and dill carefully and notes the historical confusion. The modern _vilāyatī saunf_ for anise is a clean post-classical disambiguation.]

*Brazilian Portuguese — the erva-doce question:* *erva-doce* ("sweet herb") is *ambiguous* in Brazilian Portuguese and varies by region:
- In *Northeast Brazil* (Pernambuco, Bahia, Sergipe), *erva-doce* most commonly refers to *anise* (Pimpinella anisum), especially as the seed used for sweet baking, cordials, and digestive teas.
- In *Southeast / Center-West Brazil* (São Paulo, Rio de Janeiro, Minas Gerais), *erva-doce* more often refers to *fennel* (Foeniculum vulgare) — the whole plant including the bulb.
- In *South Brazil* and *Portugal*, *erva-doce* usually refers to *fennel*.
- The clear way to disambiguate is to use the binomial.
The user-provided framing "anise (erva-doce)" follows the Northeast-Brazilian usage and is correct for *Pimpinella anisum*; this entry treats anise, with fennel treated separately.#footnote[The erva-doce ambiguity is documented in Brazilian regional cookery and ethnobotanical literature; cf. Houaiss and Cunha (etymological dictionaries) and regional cookery sources. The disambiguation by binomial is the practical professional solution.]

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

_No classical Chinese entry for Pimpinella anisum (European anise). The reader is referred to the_ fennel _entry for 小茴香 (Foeniculum vulgare) and to the standard Chinese 八角茴香 (Illicium verum, star anise — a different plant). The placement below is reasoned, not citation._

By reasoned placement on the Chinese axes — anchored on the close pharmacological similarity to fennel and star anise (all three are anethole-rich Apiaceae / Schisandraceae digestive aromatics): sweet (甘), pungent (辛); warm (溫); Spleen (脾), Stomach (胃), Lung (肺), Kidney (腎) channels by inference from the fennel / star-anise tradition. Expected actions in Chinese vocabulary: 散寒 (disperses cold), 理氣 (regulates qi), 開胃 (opens appetite), 和中 (harmonizes the middle), 止咳 (stops cough — for cold-pattern). Cautions: yin-vacuity heat, blazing fire — by the warming aromatic profile. *All interpretive, not classical Chinese attribution.*#footnote[The reasoned placement follows from the anethole-rich aromatic profile shared with fennel and star anise; the Chinese readings of 小茴香 and 八角茴香 are the appropriate cross-system templates.]

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

_Reasoned dravyaguna placement; Pimpinella anisum is not classically Sanskrit-named; what follows extends the_ madhurikā / saunf _treatment by analogy and modern phytochemistry. Explicitly interpretive._

*Reasoned rasa:* Madhura (मधुर, sweet — distinctive among the spice-fruits, due to the anethole's licorice-like sweet flavor), Kaṭu (कटु, pungent — mild).

*Reasoned guṇa:* Laghu (लघु, light), Snigdha (स्निग्ध, unctuous — from the fixed oil and aromatic resin).

*Reasoned vīrya:* Uṣṇa (उष्ण, warming).

*Reasoned vipāka:* Madhura (मधुर, sweet).#footnote[The reasoned dravyaguna profile mirrors that of fennel (madhurikā) — Bhāvaprakāśa, _Haritakyādi-varga_, _madhurikā_ entry — and is extended by analogy to anise; the slight pharmacological difference (anise is somewhat warmer and less _grāhī_ than fennel, with stronger antitussive register) is interpretive.]

*Prabhāva:* No classical assignment. The *galactagogue / lactation-promoting* action — well-attested in folk European and Brazilian tradition and modestly supported in modern clinical evidence — could be argued at prabhāva level for the closely-related fennel and is sometimes extended to anise; interpretive.

*Reasoned doṣa effect:* Pacifies Vāta and Kapha (the laghu-snigdha-uṣṇa profile); mildly Pitta-aggravating in excess.

*Reasoned karma:* _Dīpana, pācana_ (kindles digestion, supports _āma_-digestion); _vāta-anulomana_ (relieves Vāta gas and distension); _kāsa-hara, śvāsa-hara_ (relieves cough, dyspnea — for cold-pattern respiratory disease); _stanya-janana_ (lactagogue, folk and modern); _ārtava-janana_ (mild emmenagogue at higher doses); _hṛdya_ (cardiotonic, modest); _ucchvāsa-śuddhi_ (breath freshener, the classical and modern after-meal use).#footnote[Karma compiled by reasoned extension from the madhurikā / saunf treatment (Bhāvaprakāśa, _Haritakyādi-varga_) and modern phytopharmacology. Flagged as reasoned, not classical Sanskrit citation.]

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(tagged by source tier — S = core saṃhitā, N = later nighaṇṭu, F = folk/regional, M = modern/pharmacological)_

- *(F) Conditional cautions (folk Ayurvedic / Western herbal):* Pitta excess, heat-pattern reflux, high fever.
- *(M) Modern / pharmacological:*
  - *Estrogen-sensitive conditions:* trans-anethole and its dimer dianethole have weak estrogenic activity; classical European folk use of anise as a lactagogue and emmenagogue is consistent. *Caution* in known estrogen-sensitive cancers (breast, ovarian, uterine), endometriosis, fibroids.
  - *Pregnancy:* dietary use is generally considered safe; high-dose essential-oil internal use is *not* recommended in pregnancy.
  - *Anise essential oil ingestion:* the *concentrated essential oil* is irritant and can be toxic at modest doses (3–6 mL has caused symptoms); the seed and standard infusions are safe.
  - *Star-anise contamination — critical:* commercial "anise" tea/oil products have been documented to be contaminated with *Japanese star anise* (Illicium anisatum) — a *different* plant, related to star anise (I. verum) but containing *anisatin*, a potent GABA-A antagonist neurotoxin causing seizures in infants and children. The Japanese-star-anise / Chinese-star-anise contamination is a documented public-health concern, especially with herbal infant-colic teas. Pimpinella anisum (true European anise) is *not* directly contaminated by this — but anise-flavored infant products from East Asian sources may include contaminated star anise mistaken for safe star anise. *Flag explicitly: infant colic teas marketed as "anise" must come from verified Pimpinella anisum or verified Illicium verum (true Chinese star anise), not from Illicium anisatum (Japanese / shikimi).*
  - *Apiaceae allergy:* documented cross-reactivity (celery, carrot, fennel).#footnote[Trans-anethole's weak estrogenic activity: published phytopharmacology. Anise essential oil toxicity: pharmacovigilance literature. *Japanese star anise (Illicium anisatum) contamination of infant colic teas:* well documented in pediatric toxicology literature — cf. infant seizure case reports in Spain, the US, and elsewhere, leading to FDA / EFSA warnings (2003 and subsequent) about star-anise products for infant use. This is one of the more important food-safety points in the entire herb pharmacopoeia and is critical for any anise-marketed pediatric product.]

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

_Anise is canonical in Tibb — central to the carminative-and-deobstruent class alongside fennel and caraway. See_ tibb-al-arabi.typ.

*Arabic:* أنيسون (_anīsūn_) — direct from Greek _ánison_; the same word in Persian (انیسون) and Urdu (انیسون / سونف ولایتی _saunf vilāyatī_, "foreign fennel"). The Arabic preserves the Greek transmission.

*Mizāj:* *Hot and Dry in the 3rd degree* (Ibn Sīnā, *al-Qānūn* II, _anīsūn_). The strong Hot-Dry classification places anise *warmer than fennel and coriander seed* in the Tibb hierarchy of carminative aromatics.

*Af'āl:* *Mufattiḥ* (deobstruent); *kāsir li-l-riyāḥ* (carminative — the principal action); *mudirr li-l-bawl* (diuretic); *mudirr li-l-laban* (galactagogue); *mudirr li-l-ḥayḍ* (emmenagogue); *muqawwī al-mi'da*; *munaffith* (expectorant); *muḥarrik al-bāh* (aphrodisiac, modest); *muḥsin li-l-nafas* (breath-improver — the after-meal mukhavāsa register).

*Istemālāt:* flatulence and bloating; hepatic and splenic obstruction; cough with thin phlegm; lactation insufficiency; amenorrhea of cold pattern; cold-pattern impotence; urinary obstruction; dyspepsia; the after-meal breath-and-digestion register.

*Mawāne':* hot-pattern conditions; pregnancy at high-essential-oil dose; estrogen-sensitive cancers (modern caution).

*Muṣliḥ:* sugar; rose-water.

*Badal:* fennel (*rāziyānaj*); caraway (*karawiyā*).

*Tibb formulas:* *Sharbat-i Bazūrī* — fennel + anise + other diuretic seeds; *Joshānda-yi Anīsūn* — anise decoction for cough; *Khamīrah-i Gawzaban* and other Mufarriḥ-class cardiacs use anise as supporting aromatic; *ʿArq-i Anīsūn* — anise distillate; *Sufūf-i Anīsūn* — anise powder; *Maʿjūn-i Anīsūn* — anise-honey electuary for cough and respiratory weakness. The Mediterranean anise-distillate tradition (sambuca, anisette, raki, arak, ouzo) intersects the Tibb register; *arak* is documented as a medicinal preparation in some Levantine Tibb traditions, although the alcohol-based form is religiously prohibited.

*Source attestation:* Ibn Sīnā, *al-Qānūn* II, _anīsūn_; Ibn al-Bayṭār, *al-Jāmiʿ*; *Khazāyin al-Adwiya*, vol. III.#footnote[Tibb anīsūn: Avicenna and Ibn al-Bayṭār; classical Tibb-Greco-Roman direct lineage.]

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

No classical Chinese formulas use European anise (Pimpinella anisum); the closely-related but distinct Chinese drugs are 小茴香 (fennel) and 八角茴香 (star anise), treated elsewhere. Modern Chinese functional-food use of European anise is post-classical.

=== Ayurveda

No classical Sanskrit formulas use anise (Pimpinella anisum) specifically; the closely-related madhurikā / saunf (fennel) formulas — used as digestive after-meal seeds (_mukhavāsa_), in _saunf-arishta_, and as _vilāyatī-saunf_ in modern post-meal preparations — are the related register. Anise is used in modern Indian sweet-and-baked preparations and post-meal mukhavāsas.

=== Western / Greco-Roman and later European medicine

This is anise's substantive tradition.

- *Greco-Roman:* _Hippocrates_ uses anise (with some ánison / ánēthon ambiguity) in numerous prescriptions for respiratory, digestive, and gynaecological complaints. _Dioscorides_ (_De Materia Medica_ III.56, _ánison_) is the canonical entry: heating and drying, used for breath / breathing improvement, dispelling flatulence, dropsy, urination, lactation, headache, scorpion sting, sleep. _Pliny_ (_Naturalis Historia_ XX) gives a long catalogue of anise uses, attributing Pythagorean praise to it. _Galen_ classifies anise as hot and dry in the third degree.
- *Roman cookery and medicine:* anise was one of the standard *mulsum* (honey-wine) and digestive-tonic spices; the Roman wedding cake _mustaceus_ — described by Cato (_De Agri Cultura_) and Apicius — was anise-flavored, with anise-laurel garnishing; the modern Italian *biscotto / mostaccioli / anise cookie* tradition descends from this.
- *Medieval European:* anise was an officinal _quattuor semina calida minora_ ("four lesser warm seeds," with caraway, fennel, and cumin) — the canonical European carminative quartet. Used widely in monastic medicine and the Salernitan corpus.
- *Hildegard von Bingen* (_Physica_, late 12th c.) praises anise for digestion and "lightness of mood"; classical praise that connects the carminative action with the mood-elevating bitters character.
- *Galenic / pharmacy preparations:* _Aqua Anisi_ (anise-water, as carminative and weaning-time tea — a major tradition in northern-European pediatrics); _Spiritus Anisi_; _Tinctura Anisi_; _Oleum Anisi_; *Sirupus Anisi*; *Pulvis Liquiritiae Compositus* (Brown Mixture, a major 19th-c. cough syrup that includes anise oil with licorice extract and antimony). The classical European carminative-cough niche is anise's defining role.
- *Distilled spirits — the canonical anise-spirit family:* anise underwrites a major Mediterranean distilled-spirit tradition: *ouzo* (Greece), *pastis* (France), *anisette* (France / Italy), *sambuca* (Italy), *raki* (Turkey), *arak* (Levant), *mistela / aguardiente* (Iberia), *anís del mono* (Spain), Portuguese *aguardente bagaceira* with anise. The classical Greek / Roman / medieval anise tradition is the cultural root of all of these.
- *Modern Western herbal use:* persists in cough syrups, lactation teas, and digestive preparations; the European herbal pharmacopoeia (ESCOP, German Commission E) accepts anise for dyspepsia and mild cough.#footnote[Greco-Roman sources: Hippocrates _passim_; Dioscorides, _De Materia Medica_ III.56 (Beck translation, Olms-Weidmann); Pliny, _Naturalis Historia_ XX (Loeb / Rackham); Galen, _On the Properties of Foodstuffs_; Apicius, _De Re Coquinaria_; Cato, _De Agri Cultura_ on _mustaceus_. Medieval and Salernitan _quattuor semina calida minora_: standard pharmacy histories. Hildegard: _Physica_ (Throop). Galenic preparations: pharmacopoeial history; Brown Mixture (Pulvis Liquiritiae Compositus) is in the major modern pharmacopoeias of the 19th–20th c. The anise-distillate spirits tradition: cookery and beverages history.]

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

*Key constituents:*
- *Volatile oil (1.5–6% of dried fruit, the principal pharmacological form):* dominantly *trans-anethole* (~80–95% of the oil), with minor *estragole (methyl chavicol)*, *anisaldehyde*, *anisic acid*, *p-anisic alcohol*, *fenchone* (in trace), *limonene*, *linalool*, *γ-himachalene*. The trans-anethole content is the principal active.
- *Fixed oil* (~10–30% of the dried fruit).
- *Coumarins*: scopoletin, umbelliferone (modest).
- *Flavonoids*: rutin, apigenin glycosides, luteolin glycosides.
- *Phenolic acids*: chlorogenic acid.#footnote[Phytochemistry: standard pharmacognosy (Evans, _Trease and Evans' Pharmacognosy_); ESCOP and Commission E monographs on Anisi fructus; reviews in _Phytochemistry_ and _Journal of Essential Oil Research_.]

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Carminative / antispasmodic:* trans-anethole has well-documented antispasmodic activity on intestinal smooth muscle; the modern correlate of the Dioscoridean and classical European carminative use. Clinical evidence in functional dyspepsia and IBS is supportive.
- *Antitussive / expectorant:* anethole and anisaldehyde have expectorant activity; the basis of the cough-syrup tradition (Brown Mixture, Pulvis Liquiritiae Compositus).
- *Galactagogue:* mild weak-estrogenic activity of trans-anethole; folk lactation-tea use has modest modern support; effect sizes small.
- *Mild estrogenic / emmenagogue:* the weak phytoestrogenic activity of anethole's dimer dianethole and related compounds; classical use as emmenagogue has biochemical basis.
- *Antimicrobial / antifungal:* anethole has broad in vitro activity; clinical relevance modest.
- *Antioxidant:* standard for the phenolic / flavonoid fraction.
- *Insecticidal / pediculicide:* anise oil has been used in lice/mite preparations; clinical evidence modest.
- *Estragole genotoxicity caveat:* estragole (a minor constituent of anise oil) has been shown to be genotoxic and weakly carcinogenic in rodent models at high doses; EU regulation has limited estragole content in food. In whole-seed and standard culinary use, estragole exposure is negligible; in *high-dose chronic essential-oil use*, the consideration is real, but is dwarfed by other considerations (e.g. the Japanese-star-anise contamination concern is much more important clinically).#footnote[Pharmacology and clinical evidence: ESCOP monograph on Anisi fructus and Anisi aetheroleum; German Commission E; current systematic reviews in _Phytotherapy Research_, _Phytomedicine_. Estragole concern: EFSA opinion on estragole and methyleugenol; the regulatory limits apply to high-extract use, not seed/infusion use.]

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:*
- *Culinary / infusion use:* up to ~3 g of seed per day in food and tea is safe and the dietary register; the dominant use.
- *Essential oil (Oleum Anisi):* in pharmaceutical preparation as flavoring (mg quantities) and as a cough-syrup component (low single-mL doses, well-diluted); *not for direct ingestion* of multi-mL quantities.
- *ESCOP / Commission E:* 0.05–0.2 g essential oil per day (cough), 3 g seed per day (digestive).
- *Risk groups (use cautiously or avoid):* (a) known *estrogen-sensitive cancer* or *endometriosis* (theoretical caution); (b) *pregnancy* at high-essential-oil doses (culinary safe); (c) *infants* — *do not use star-anise products of unverified species origin* (Japanese star anise contamination); (d) *Apiaceae allergy*; (e) heavy chronic essential-oil use (estragole concern); (f) hormone-sensitive contexts in concert with hormone therapy.
- *Reference figures, not personalized medical advice.*#footnote[Dose ranges: ESCOP monograph; Commission E monograph; WHO _Monographs_. Risk groups and the critical infant / Japanese-star-anise caveat (above). Reference thresholds, not personalized medical advice.]

